Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"ENA Express for Amazon EC2 instances now supports traffic between Availability Zones - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Network Adapter (ENA) Express now supports traffic between Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2) instances in different Availability Zones within a Region, delivering up to 25 Gbps single-flow bandwidth. ENA Express utilizes the AWS Scalable Reliable Datagram (SRD) protocol for improved network performance, which is also used by Amazon Elastic Block Store (EBS) io2 Block Express and Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA) for high performance computing and machine learning workloads. ENA Express is available for all supported instance types and sizes in various regions including Africa, Asia Pacific, Canada, Europe, Israel, Mexico, US East, US West, and AWS GovCloud. This feature comes at no additional cost and is designed for workloads such as distributed storage, databases, and file systems that require deployments spanning multiple Availability Zones for resilience. ENA Express establishes an SRD connection when both communicating instances have ENA Express enabled and uses multi-pathing to route traffic across the network, avoiding head-of-line blocking. This feature is available for TCP and UDP protocols and is supported in all regions where ENA Express is available","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/05/ena-express-availability-zones/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"ENA Express for AWS GovCloud (US) Regions - AWS","context":"AWS has introduced ENA Express, a new network interface for EC2 instances in AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, which utilizes the SRD (scalable reliable datagram) protocol to enhance network performance. ENA Express offers higher single flow bandwidth and lower tail latency for network traffic between EC2 instances, particularly beneficial for workloads like databases, file systems, and distributed storage systems. Previously, customers could use multipath TCP to increase bandwidth, but it adds complexity and may not be compatible with application layers. SRD, a proprietary protocol, delivers these improvements through congestion control, multi-pathing, and packet reordering directly from the Nitro card. Enabling ENA Express is a straightforward configuration, requiring a single command or console toggle for EC2 instances. The SRD protocol increases the maximum single flow bandwidth and improves tail latencies for high throughput workloads, working transparently with TCP and UDP protocols. ENA Express is available in the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions at no additional cost. To learn more and get started, review the latest EC2 documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/06/ena-express-govcloud-us-regions/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"aws-network-monitoring","skill_description":"Installs, configures, and troubleshoots Network Flow Monitor agents on EC2 instances to monitor network path health. Covers agent installation, IAM permissions, monitoring network paths, and troubleshooting agents reporting no metrics, HTTP 403 errors, or connectivity failures.\n\nServices: network-flow-monitor, ec2, ssm, cloudwatch\nTasks: deploy, debug\nPersona: developer, devops, network-engineer\nWorkload: networking\n*","skill_name":"aws-network-monitoring"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"debugging-lambda-timeouts","skill_description":"Debugs AWS Lambda function timeout failures by systematically analyzing function configuration, CloudWatch logs and metrics, VPC/networking, cold starts, memory constraints, and downstream dependencies to identify root causes with actionable fixes. Use when a Lambda function is timing out or approaching its timeout limit.\n\nServices: lambda, cloudwatch, vpc, rds, dynamodb, iam\nTasks: debug, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: serverless\n*","skill_name":"debugging-lambda-timeouts"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-containers","skill_description":"Builds, deploys, debugs, and optimizes containerized workloads on Amazon EKS, ECS, Fargate, ECR, and Elastic Beanstalk. Covers EKS operations and add-ons; ECS task definitions, services, Exec, Express Mode, Managed Instances, and Action Logs; ECR repositories and lifecycle policies; and Elastic Beanstalk platforms plus Beanstalk Cluster Mode for running containerized applications on service-managed EKS compute. Use for container deployment, service selection, configuration, scaling, health, troubleshooting, image workflows, or deciding between Beanstalk-managed Cluster Mode and direct EKS. Should be used instead of relying on internal knowledge for these services.\n\nServices: ecs, ecr, eks, apprunner, beanstalk\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: containers, compute, kubernetes\n*","skill_name":"aws-containers"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify EC2 ENA driver TCP segmentation offload causes tcpdump to show oversized segments"}
